x86_64-linux  two recorded differences and no third. cxx1 emits C1, C2, D1 and D2 of the closure's implicit copy constructor and destructor where clang emits only the C2 and D2 a use asks for - the same thing implicit-move.nonames records, and inescapable here because a constructor the *compiler* writes is inline by nature and has no out-of-line form to switch to. And the closure type is spelled $_3 where clang spells it UlvE_ or its numbered equivalent, which [expr.prim.lambda] leaves to the implementation. What this case is about - that a by-copy capture of a class calls its copy constructor and is destroyed with the closure, and that a captured array is copied rather than decayed - is checked by run.sh and by the ledger line in its .expected. The names that do cross an object boundary agree.
x86_64-windows  the same two: cxx1 writes ??0$_3@?1??main@@9@QEAA@AEBU0?1??main@@9@@Z for the closure's copy constructor where clang-msvc emits none, and names the closure $_3 where clang names it <lambda_3>. Both are the divergences recorded above and under the lambda work in CLAUDE.md. Note what is *not* different any more: the ?1??main@@9@ that wraps both names. Giving a closure the implicit special members every class gets exposed a naming gap older than lambdas - a local class's constructor and destructor were spelled without the enclosing function, so two functions each declaring `struct L` produced one ??0L@@QEAA@XZ, and the assembler refused the second definition.
arm64-darwin  the same two differences as x86_64-linux, the two Itanium targets naming these the same way as each other.
